#{[{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125354} :activity/id #uuid "881d7f99-9cf9-5cc5-ac3e-817c6cfcbee4" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125354 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125766 :tutorial/content "Given two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V,$ the **change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$** is the matrix $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nIn expanded form, the matrix looks as follows:\n![](images/06/062187705e6074afa6b5356be64f8deb878be540e2c3b236b22dbd244b872cc6.svg)\n\nFor example, let's consider two bases $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}, \\qquad\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}.\n$$\n\nIf we happen to know that\n\n![](images/32/32409a45b5ebcf6b8c1cc43404126b514066ace8d8c71bcec2e99f759833ec84.svg) \n\nthen the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & \\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 6\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\  & \\,{↑}_{[{b}_{1}]_{C}}\\,{↑}_{[{b}_{2}]_{C}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe beauty of the change-of-coordinates matrix is that it allows us to express the vector $[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}$ in the basis $\\mathcal C$ via the transformation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal C} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot [\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}.\n$$\n\nThis can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede6eb17f0667a8974902bf2d181150eeb3c665faa3059bb818bb9a61b631511.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "46d12d06-cf6a-5c5d-bb38-8670b3501be4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3213 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7241b03-69a5-5e64-ac19-86478024cf90" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125356}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125356 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566"} :step/id #uuid "bd858f5c-ef37-5df3-babd-34538f6f381c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125357}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125357 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124492 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc"} :step/id #uuid "80c4bbb9-f58d-5db9-9875-a6de44dddc60" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125358}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125358 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125767 :tutorial/content "Since the columns of a change-of-coordinates matrix are linearly independent, the matrix must be invertible.\n\nIn fact, the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}\n\t\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f2fa55fd-270b-5d4e-89e4-ec0fe2c4e0c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3215 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "e23604b2-7d9d-5701-83b0-ef4dd1b95b45" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125359}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125359 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124493 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3"} :step/id #uuid "f4b1b6b1-2c84-53dc-b2e9-6ae8617800c7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125501}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125360 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124494 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f989cbaf-2d99-5267-8864-48a48db2d535"} :step/id #uuid "a7664106-d8c0-540f-9ff4-1553bdb9d7a4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125823 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we have bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ and we want to find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ denoted $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nRecall that the columns of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C},$ that is\n![](images/fb/fb63e6dcb2c7b0f8417e4f3fd394d42fa4bb84017552779903c40e4114639ac6.svg)\n\nTo compute $P_{\\small{\\color{black}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{black}\\mathcal{C}}},$ we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/64/644f78998195b8be26cb21bf5d680fbebed808d8f412769f9a3b06901533e87d.svg)\n\nIf we reduce the left-hand side of $M$ to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ will emerge on the right-hand side:\n\n![](images/52/52f73b32e9700813960a9c544b71fedcce865ed3c452094be36615459663be9d.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "a6e71bf0-0662-5ac2-abc9-4edf7b085968" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4439 :tutorial/title "Computing the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Using Row-Reduction"} :step/id #uuid "49ef4ed6-562f-5c33-91e3-3f02216c2e4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125360}}] :activity/title "The Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124721} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426288} :activity/id #uuid "17862355-72a6-5fb8-967b-537e8771192b" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426287 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394305 :question/id #uuid "a27a3c1e-ad3c-56e4-aa1a-d2aedbc7058d"} :step/id #uuid "047626a4-44a1-518d-a9b8-705468162bf9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426289}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426288 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426286 :question/id #uuid "b83d51a4-64e8-5d15-b4eb-1a14ddbe497b"} :step/id #uuid "14b98c73-3778-5d7d-bd8f-d1f3c481dbdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426287}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426289 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426285 :question/id #uuid "abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913"} :step/id #uuid "2efe5e7e-1009-5580-b1b3-51c076c0ff38" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426291}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247362 :question/id #uuid "cb921b26-a20c-5f23-a094-49ac99b2c6bd"} :step/id #uuid "43b3afd1-0b21-5d59-9760-22f54f558dd0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426291 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247349 :question/id #uuid "6524c152-aeba-5139-803b-30e61293f48c"} :step/id #uuid "c97d309f-4d0e-5752-9011-015efc23599f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426290}}] :activity/title "The Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426272}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050008 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124491 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124304 :question/id #uuid "4c1e01c1-d607-5ad8-8643-b3317201ab9d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4832" :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ If $\\mathbf{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2,$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](images/da/daf4111656a525abf51bf58105831ed11378ed7fc52915e1e58ca5f75884bcf0.svg)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](images/be/bed6471ce9aed8eb16a0d35f1d9985b8e99941f7c1326645de971960bdb5aedd.svg)"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "595fd527-e8fd-5360-87f1-acbeab511566" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124895 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89936cf5-c061-52b5-9323-ac37e40f0168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121971} {:answer/id #uuid "9899b137-f857-5892-b812-d661fec42559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121972} {:answer/id #uuid "aea62c1d-df91-5d27-82b6-367a29d9fecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121973} {:answer/id #uuid "4b42152c-d451-51fd-9ebd-a6f736e5d189" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121974} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8e0b98-dbab-503f-b571-fd37953d6bbf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121975}] :answer-field/id #uuid "01d04dca-5187-5ca2-8ada-ffb51f661dda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121970}] :question/id #uuid "3f0b072a-5cea-534d-83b7-ad5b5b8c7990" :question/math-academy-id "q-35685" :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ and $C = {c_{1}, c_{2}}$ be bases of $R^{2}$.\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{B → C}$ from $B$ to $C$ given that\n\n$[b_{1}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, [b_{2}]_{C} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125228 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520cf59b-0e8a-5135-a5bb-58dbfaac9d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349192} {:answer/id #uuid "63a6a38a-272b-5ef8-ac5d-9b8a62db6e68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349359} {:answer/id #uuid "a929d2cb-f110-55b5-829c-d6e3d6ec6f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349954} {:answer/id #uuid "b73754bf-f54b-5788-b870-664463b6849c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350069} {:answer/id #uuid "efee334f-bad5-5592-a240-43676034e441" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350545} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8f9c2f-e58d-54c9-bff6-90fe41f9bb30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350897}] :question/id #uuid "8ff70514-6088-5578-a352-2d83d0571687" :question/math-academy-id "q-81394" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}-{c}_{3}, \\\\ {b}_{2} & =5{c}_{3}+2{c}_{2}, \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={c}_{2}-{c}_{1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ a & b & 1 \\\\ -1 & c & 0\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{b}_{3}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13508c3a-b778-5da9-944c-a0e84a3dd9b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321297} {:answer/id #uuid "8cf26bc4-131a-5b43-b454-72c6b6e9630d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322272} {:answer/id #uuid "923588e2-7ae8-5837-ae1d-a537b62e5d60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322308} {:answer/id #uuid "f37ec7a1-c181-53d6-8795-c6c53eebd62e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323059} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e0f297-0165-5f20-bbd5-d9e2c5c3d348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323059} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7f66aa0-94fa-514c-baa2-1e24130ae6ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323593}] :question/id #uuid "b80daa7e-24b9-50c1-a8b6-05bfcb214049" :question/math-academy-id "q-36355" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $C=\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ If ${b}_{1}=5{c}_{2}-3{c}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}=2{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2},$ find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $B$ relative to the basis $C$ are\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\,[{b}_{2}]_{C}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351377 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41e4b858-0939-54cf-9dda-08263bf19bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4ebaf4-f6ae-5d83-b8f7-d86c8df809a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349136} {:answer/id #uuid "677d8855-5a11-57ef-aca3-3b105f83a4d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-5,$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349398} {:answer/id #uuid "6ffbd4c9-7f71-5f70-aa18-f035562c2e34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=4,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349475} {:answer/id #uuid "8948bc51-f3d5-5f6c-9eff-8fbe6334f755" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=3,$ $b=0,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349694}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349044} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf2b4e0-ef90-544d-9360-0816914a3219" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350925}] :question/id #uuid "25384ce4-a891-57c8-b633-4441e98187a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-81825" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ and $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}}$ be bases of ${R}^{3}.$ \nWhat are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =3{b}_{3}-{b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =4{b}_{2}-5{b}_{1}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =2{b}_{1}+3{b}_{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & c\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $C$ relative to the basis $B$ are\n\n$$\n[{c}_{1}]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{c}_{2}]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,[{c}_{3}]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=4,$ and $c=3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386640 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c074f1f1-190d-5c2b-be44-47f1519f9e18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386642} {:answer/id #uuid "428736dc-252f-56df-b2cf-058df8e2f2b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386643} {:answer/id #uuid "4293ab9b-7451-59be-b692-d3049e359a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386644} {:answer/id #uuid "a593476a-084b-51fd-b813-f788df67a1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386645} {:answer/id #uuid "dba8598e-a9a8-5938-a8b8-24e380b6f380" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"