_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 11 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 11 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386689 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8825f14c-9a42-524d-9c28-29837aebedb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386691} {:answer/id #uuid "98835778-342b-5c02-8d88-888de609e677" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386692} {:answer/id #uuid "d94196f9-3ecd-5be0-83e8-bcc999375295" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386693} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee547c-4202-5153-9e7d-5620efcc2bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386694} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9a6ec6-14f6-58f7-bc3c-2ea967387600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatri