e the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 3 & -9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1100f1bc-ed34-5a67-be8b-4cd9cbd023d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} {:answer/id #uuid "50b04112-6a4b-5de0-8b36-cac7c5a0a40d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416544} {:answer/id #uuid "5a88c79a-d8b2-5e43-8a47-5013ebd4e46e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416548} {:answer/id #uuid "945a35b8-1727-56f0-8d1f-a7a7ff35591a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416564} {:answer/id #uuid "a2edcdc2-4bd9-58b5-8e0b-973a0e0009a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416527} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ec3541-b791-5354-9296-f775e0ff2635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416585}] :question/id #uuid "4f239f11-8e8b-53a2-a96f-22cc233efad1" :question/math-academy-id "q-113526" :question/problem "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40f45c93-6fe2-5f26-851c-9bd122ebd2a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416541} {:answer/id #uuid "8fca4fa8-84ec-5284-b191-c15918cd020b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416562} {:answer/id #uuid "b825f629-82ac-53f3-b87f-12a36ddac7c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416572} {:answer/id #uuid "caa59a06-8d44-5e97-8650-fe3fd7d19fda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416577} {:answer/id #uuid "f89b032a-28b2-5d9f-9d76-0bd18854f341" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416583} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a77bdea-a6bc-5acf-8e6b-c3386884ffde" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416590}] :question/id #uuid "699ab275-8547-554b-97d2-1ddcd2846071" :question/math-academy-id "q-113508" :question/problem "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}$ and $C={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416604 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b26590d-792e-51f9-b6ee-9b92d34732ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416538} {:answer/id #uuid "3c3ac9c4-89a8-53e0-9f5b-0281ad0e261f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416539} {:answer/id #uuid "419a7dcc-0ec9-5201-aaac-a0d26fd4a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416542} {:answer/id #uuid "57bd0aaf-7e37-5d1f-aa28-5056aba60ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416546} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f96822-b423-5c09-9a06-dd548ce5f549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416574}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416574} :answer-field/id #uuid "dbb6c09b-dbdd-53f8-b483-27b433b6c2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416594}] :question/id #uuid "d9fbd56e-ebad-5e95-b783-252de92d505c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36358" :question/problem "Let $B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $S$ is the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426285 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13aa3648-2a29-5251-b63d-d4b7f8b86f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426273} {:answer/id #uuid "4ec21016-7e3e-5b63-aa64-0f06d50c5c24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426277} {:answer/id #uuid "58f4f17b-6aad-55e7-b1d1-014e99ce6351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426278} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2f609f-5e0d-57ff-8535-d184b581b518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426281} {:answer/id #uuid "d0371c07-7afd-5a4b-8c13-05d0d0aba1ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426281} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce86fa9-4f61-508b-a2b4-29ba44037897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426283}] :question/id #uuid "abe1f7cc-b85d-54fe-b1a8-40e514a9b913" :question/math-academy-id "q-81906" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ and $C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 8 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c3b89-889a-5113-860d-9f442b0f7b5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434936} {:answer/id #uuid "753fb253-4ce3-57c6-9f54-6bb8fae24d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434939} {:answer/id #uuid "a20fd614-e995-59f1-8191-fd45b0182f75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434941} {:answer/id #uuid "a2788a1d-e2ab-584c-86fe-21bbcb38e3ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434942} {:answer/id #uuid "c8e82d70-a589-5d2a-9e35-2278a91f4109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434942} :answer-field/id #uuid "0710a289-e926-5692-b8d2-c99ac59e739f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186434948}] :question/id #uuid "f9a03134-8525-58b4-a224-da8d571c355f" :question/math-academy-id "q-113519" :question/problem "Let $B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ and $C=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as $\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}$ and $\\{{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Between Two Bases"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1928 :topic/title "The Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847} :activity/id #uuid "f83b8c51-727f-5eb0-9f6f-bcc3b133cfd6" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163222 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **symmetric** if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA^T = A\n$$\n\nEquivalently, an $n\\times n$ matrix is symmetric if $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $1 \\leq i,j\\leq n.$\n\nIf $A$ is symmetric, this really means that it is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/0f/0f62a89f8da1ab7bfb86d46fddb441e95e5e3a9554e5a88662691b4df40e19af.svg)\n\nis symmetric since $A^T = A.$ \n\nOn the other hand, the matrix \n\n![](images/78/780de3c3f66b1037e4152224f4aa98b65b8b1b859adfe7c3881bb830d7eecd66.svg)\n\nis not symmetric. Indeed, since $b_{12} \\neq b_{21},$ we have $B^T \\neq B.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fe9d8bc-6133-5ab4-8b4f-af2b40817e02" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4880 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9c19c64f-0597-5273-8ff3-50729d7a67fc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368"} :step/id #uuid "f2c61bc7-19cc-5e6a-a533-0450e49aa5dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163254 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a symmetric matrix. Then, we have the following properties:\n\n- $-A$ is symmetric\n\n- $A^m$ is symmetric for any positive integer $m$\n\n- $A^{-1}$ is symmetric for nonsingular $A$\n\nIf $A$ and $B$ are $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices, we have the following properties:\n\n- $A+B$ is symmetric\n\n- The product $A\\cdot B$ is *not* always symmetric. For example, if \n![](images/e4/e41d28b6c6562ada530024fe6ab29cc16447dce14b2f741b8b35758618c78103.svg)\nthen the product $A\\cdot B$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich is *not* symmetric.\n\nFinally, the following two properties can be used to construct symmetric matrices:\n\n- $A + A^T$ is symmetric for any *square* matrix $A$\n\n- $A^T A$ and $A A^T$ are symmetric for *any* matrix $A$\n\nThe last property is true for *any* $m\\times n$ matrix. This property is particularly useful, as we'll discover in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "c20572b7-1e75-5be2-a39c-b2327caff234" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5043 :tutorial/title "Properties of Symmetric Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "a119eca9-e695-53d9-9049-eef3212a6e22" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162925 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79"} :step/id #uuid "dd82fa90-8f6a-5c31-a6bd-faa30ff2ee22"}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162214} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156} :activity/id #uuid "4147fa8b-5ff8-579d-afae-acd5982a7b2d" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360684 :question/id #uuid "a6c182c5-819c-5206-a031-9309a3557f8d"} :step/id #uuid "13d711fe-4f91-5fa4-8a59-b8df54fc9061" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/id #uuid "225f8bdf-5da4-5137-973e-d6466e5f7f7a"} :step/id #uuid "850fd871-11f1-5a64-9c68-516ffbeed054" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425154 :question/id #uuid "ea940e2f-7cf1-5b69-9241-9cde37e5f341"} :step/id #uuid "ae65fa76-f15c-5a90-8603-42143fe53ed6"}] :activity/title "Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425147}] :db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021 :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161771 :question/id #uuid "c68d70a2-e4f8-5f08-8220-3df420a1e199" :question/math-academy-id "e-8156" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n![](images/fb/fb428c39bbc03be798bca2463658676de5c684396001cf547e9395352f456bc9.svg)" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA = A^T\n$$\n\nEquivalently, $a_{ij} = a_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P=P^T.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q=Q^T.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since $r_{12} \\neq r_{21},$ we obtain $R \\neq R^T.$\n![](images/3f/3f91ec40e717eac93bb38f9c13f7b1700e84ac109ccceaffbb8473ba8d6b4e34.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08e8db0d-99fb-5ea7-9318-4ca99d58e090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354745} {:answer/id #uuid "08fcbe18-d45e-5004-92c0-3a6422d2d1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354747} {:answer/id #uuid "828f3975-2835-5574-9c59-f895cdfa0945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355811} {:answer/id #uuid "82d2f3a8-d1ef-544d-accc-41592fb63fef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355815} {:answer/id #uuid "a7339d78-1022-501f-b997-37e63b3a3c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354747} :answer-field/id #uuid "66133d70-b9fb-5d32-b845-e980e242563c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357093}] :question/id #uuid "dd69c0a4-182b-55d3-9077-6d410397ebbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-93583" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56ba5482-5def-5eb1-8afb-b35147f01cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460395} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b55bdd-c3cc-58fc-a9bf-8d537f751a4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460396} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5900cb9-3c0a-5858-84ac-a5057863b2eb" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461064} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "426fd1ef-0fc7-5598-a5b3-9ce88b4d09c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460397} {:answer/id #uuid "72b04334-21e6-5b43-a89a-7fc2f082bcfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460397} :answer-field/id #uuid "62aa287c-82c9-5b53-bffa-feef2459476d" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461065} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc940bfb-2218-5131-b425-d004e50900ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460399} {:answer/id #uuid "97f47d98-81cc-56ed-ad89-84f36eb470e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460400} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e781ce-f91c-5253-aa0a-4f69bb5ac8d2" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461066}] :question/id #uuid "4f59c753-3356-5eb7-a27a-598f27f0d76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93994" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 8 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{12}\\ne {q}_{21},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $R$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12857ff7-7f34-53f2-92a8-fc5552e5223d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269431} {:answer/id #uuid "18c99cf2-a2ea-5550-8e35-29afed4e9f0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269476} {:answer/id #uuid "555c78b8-9307-5a0d-84c0-e46552fd5cce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270027} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d9d61f-bacc-5381-9864-7b41873c8c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} {:answer/id #uuid "c47e9ae5-65c0-542a-acd0-bd7144abd9f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,$ $Q,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270998} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7ed00bf-fcba-544c-8d6c-e3b5291f4354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271844}] :question/id #uuid "180f854f-d44c-5145-855a-5e70b4193e38" :question/math-academy-id "q-140504" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $R={R}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P,$ $Q,$ and $R.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa67ce65-42d8-53e7-8c99-76f56fc9bcce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273859} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3ce2af-19e4-5fa6-bb1c-90f4bceacb0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274024} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c641db-c970-54c3-9db3-c50ea1207234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274401} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa91c1-4798-5ba0-80a6-7ee8a99088ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274435} {:answer/id #uuid "efe3bafc-8625-52ac-b13b-f6e88685587e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the matrices are symmetric" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274401} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba0e3b07-f917-5e4e-bc53-42a8b1e9f4f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274958}] :question/id #uuid "550ab27b-8963-569e-bb9e-df189c98b4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-143679" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${p}_{13}\\ne {p}_{31},$ we obtain $P\\ne {P}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{13}\\ne {q}_{31},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the matrices are symmetric.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275134 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "691d2386-08e0-5e84-8528-ca09e15fee56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464300} {:answer/id #uuid "40fc8e5d-a7c6-5dee-ad15-ced14c3143c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464301} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe6f4042-36ad-5790-9b6a-ec08b4a4e1de" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465084} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a166b78e-fbfa-54cb-b123-2bd64adb1d28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464302} {:answer/id #uuid "aaf7898e-f1aa-583f-936b-db61e1a09fba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464303} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d8b8c80-ca14-520f-b6c2-4799187a9aeb" :answer-field/key "Q" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465085} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d54f34b-efcc-5076-9803-a07b0f77a6b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464304} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf8d2b6-60b1-5f51-852c-b6a4df097275" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464304} :answer-field/id #uuid "671893c7-dec8-5832-a276-3876dd47a359" :answer-field/key "R" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465086}] :question/id #uuid "2475e8ae-4517-52f5-bd41-fe3d91352945" :question/math-academy-id "q-143680" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 11 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 11 & -9 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357611 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b67bf2d-f434-5cbf-a65f-fe92576ed3fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469638} {:answer/id #uuid "1f304f6a-e85b-5ca9-8056-745663c7ef23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469638} :answer-field/id #uuid "04035bc7-0baa-52a2-8819-5785c4397711" :answer-field/key "P" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470431} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a25bb71a-4230-5f5d-aaf6-2df76c7f39c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469640} {:answer/id #uuid "82cc5f65-1d57-56f6-b5fe-cc38b670cf45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 1759