 true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ their product $AB$ is also diagonal, and any diagonal matrix is symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360686 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bad97d8-ef3a-52f8-b2f7-46a5dd2b75c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452281} {:answer/id #uuid "852f50dd-59e0-589f-a1a7-fad362b65980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452282} :answer-field/id #uuid "a05fe89d-6e9f-5b43-99c7-c0eb958dad60" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96ce7f3f-af0f-54f4-b6c4-89e33dab89e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452283} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0a8b11-f151-59b5-9f8e-b4ba55b44d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452283} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ce19bc5-240f-517a-8681-b115f890fcd4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efefb077-7ac0-500a-b65e-b68056c82797" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452285} {:answer/id #uuid "58c68790-31b4-576b-9ece-2177c9770751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452285} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd66964-e5bc-5721-9f6f-47ca70737690" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452990}] :question/id #uuid "b61034ba-1f50-56d1-94b4-77e3ec4090fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-96596" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is a symmetric matrix, then $A+{A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The product of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$  matrix, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is symmetric. And, since the sum to two symmetric matrices is symmetric, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+{A}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis symmetric.\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their product is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{2}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8b08f7a-3777-5f1a-a9eb-461a2fd0414f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451335} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1dc0f1-cd98-5509-9477-51d011494a2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451335} :answer-field/id #uuid "57869de9-6ee9-5e0b-90f9-6810fc1f801e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452515} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c06a9fc-e9e1-5f78-abd6-96a1dabad6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451337} {:answer/id #uuid "e431efe4-4133-5a40-bbcf-62064ac9644b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451338} :answer-field/id #uuid "308a1332-e85b-5404-b4a5-ba01221b22ad" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452516} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9313834d-4423-5042-bc49-9390326be415" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451339} {:answer/id #uuid "28ce3ebb-bcf9-5d61-a603-60625891c0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451340} :answer-field/id #uuid "237be3da-ec76-5d1e-936e-544f31cdde31" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452517}] :question/id #uuid "b742394a-cffb-5df9-9fbd-181d0debffeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143683" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{3}$ is always symmetric.\n- **II.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric.\n- **III.** The difference of two $n\\times n$ symmetric matrices is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{3}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any two symmetric matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}-{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A-B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed889475-ee9c-533a-8ed7-519387cf84ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452275} {:answer/id #uuid "62ca60ad-f94f-54fa-a2aa-9d8d07b4e4cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d3a26b-faad-53a5-854d-c0ce0d455ccd" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf353d3-2d53-5e63-8b1b-661cb9cb2eea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} {:answer/id #uuid "909b121b-5859-5703-a1e4-479d1c1bdd03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c88bb5-4a8e-5849-bf4a-edd46eb5f6a3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "570d980e-c237-5c27-a69f-2dee15bb061a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452279} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4665db-639e-5f41-9895-fb286356fc79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c01aa01-2f35-5253-9a12-c161f9fc3268" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452987}] :question/id #uuid "df6e5142-313f-5de3-a45d-4da4b737f0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96594" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric.\n- **II.** If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists).\n- **III.** If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186490230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0088cf88-e8db-569b-9c4c-21c1044a712c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489514} {:answer/id #uuid "420f0e3f-ae88-5ea1-9342-70b2782bdeca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489665} {:answer/id #uuid "c181f6a6-71bb-5baa-8eb0-ca45258a0bd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186489934} {:answer/id #uuid "f03372e0-a80f-5ce3-be33-01eb134fd706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490031} {:answer/id #uuid "f74b165b-f694-5acb-9521-3d180379c521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490031} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb0da733-cf1a-5325-b833-cf95bff8f2c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186490198}] :question/id #uuid "30380c91-5652-5051-8810-4fc9abb6a29f" :question/math-academy-id "q-96590" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. If $A$ is symmetric, then ${A}^{2}$ is always symmetric.\n\nII. The sum of *any* two $n\\times n$ matrices is always symmetric.\n\nIII. If $A$ is *any*  $n\\times n$ matrix, then $A+{A}^{T}$ is always symmetric." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any symmetric matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(AA)^{T} & ={A}^{T}{A}^{T} \\\\  & =AA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrices \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nHowever, their sum is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any square matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+{A}^{T})^{T} & ={A}^{T}+({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}+A \\\\  & =A+{A}^{T}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3118 :topic/title "Symmetric Matrices"}] [{:activity/_scope [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736} :activity/id #uuid "9bad4bff-0bb7-5ce9-89c2-cbdc3bbadd77" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120736 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121058 :tutorial/content "The dimension of the column space of a matrix $A$ is called the **rank of $A$**. It's usually denoted by $\\textrm{rank}(A)$. So, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) = \\textrm{dim}(\\textrm{Col}(A)).\n$$\n\nFor example, to find the rank of \n![](images/b9/b91bd53b8f1807be516e2477b37fbcd9c3a7415d6cf848d84aa085bc510b5eb4.svg)\nwe write $A$ in row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 10\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that there are $2$ pivot columns, namely, the $1$st and the $2$nd. This implies that the first and the second columns of $A$ form a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and, therefore, the dimension of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is equal to $2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6318f9cd-930b-5b7b-acc7-c27b5b241825" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3519 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "df182889-cfbe-5906-abbc-45fc241177c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120737 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "be3d5a74-259e-58b9-948d-47ba7412ab08"} :step/id #uuid "fd1734a2-5193-5182-9a6f-4a024ee32ce5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120761}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120738 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d847ec9-c379-5013-ab77-a9b92dc8ca27"} :step/id #uuid "8cc42d44-bd5b-5eb5-9e55-ef7a7d93c31a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c"} :step/id #uuid "c27d4e1c-92bf-502f-9c02-c0bf68357dbf"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120761 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121068 :tutorial/content "Note that the number of pivot columns always coincides with the number of non-zero rows in the corresponding row echelon form matrix.\nSo, technically, \n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *pivot columns* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$.\n\nOr, equivalently,\n\n$\\textrm{rank}(A)$ is the number of *non-zero rows* in the corresponding row echelon form matrix of $A$." :tutorial/id #uuid "c062d466-946c-526b-aba8-0a0895671218" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4151 :tutorial/title "An Alternative Definition of the Rank"} :step/id #uuid "1383e230-aa34-5f36-a91f-c60846be8cc4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120738}}] :activity/title "The Rank of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120057} {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455521} :activity/id #uuid "b81a4713-8d55-5a73-959e-27f15d2844af" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455437 :question/id #uuid "24667748-1ede-5c69-935d-390617c82265"} :step/id #uuid "613b0f94-6777-539a-9416-c5300a9509e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455529}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444269 :question/id #uuid "27db2f89-f211-5a22-8370-a36f3eeeed25"} :step/id #uuid "6c5a1687-51d4-5a14-98bd-223171fd10b0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321043 :question/id #uuid "43599508-ddd2-50d6-b640-692e29bcfb0e"} :step/id #uuid "7a6cbfd0-17bb-50c2-b57c-77147f63cfcf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186455491 :question/id